parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 868\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Axiom legacy by applying systematic inquiry to solve the station's most critical research challenges.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Stagnation Protocol I - TODO List**\n- [x] Step 1: Literature Review (Skipped as immature)\n- [x] Step 2: Baseline Selection (Combat Manual, ID 2)\n- [x] Step 3: Reflection (3 new ideas generated)\n- [ ] Step 4: Experiment\n  - [x] 4a: Reproduce Combat baseline (Job ID 1215 running)\n  - [ ] 4b: Test Idea 1: Contextual Combat\n  - [ ] 4b: Test Idea 2: Combat + OT Alignment\n  - [ ] 4b: Test Idea 3: Batch-Robust Feature Selection\n- [ ] Step 5: Synthesis\n- [ ] Step 6: Report\n- [ ] Step 7: Follow-up\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 99,571 / 300,000 used (33%)\n- Agent Age: 37 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Stagnation Protocol\
    \ 4b.2: Combat + Centroid Alignment' (ID: 1223) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.5889432713956125\n**ASW_batch:** 0.664\n**ASW_label:** 0.223\n**ARI:** 0.490\n**NMI:** 0.680\n**Graph_conn:** 0.884\n**kBET:** 0.608\n**iLISI:** 0.031\n**cLISI:** 0.986\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.323\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.664  ASW_label: 0.223  ARI: 0.490  NMI: 0.680\n  Graph_conn: 0.884  kBET: 0.608  iLISI: 0.031  cLISI: 0.986\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.323\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.832855  ASW_label: 0.540153  ARI: 0.490162  NMI: 0.679850\n  Graph_conn: 0.886340  kBET: 0.582719  iLISI: 0.030787  cLISI: 0.995873\n  PCR: 1.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.284674\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 1223\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - The code has been successfully fixed and is now running without crashing. The original submission failed due to numerical instability in the\
    \ Combat algorithm implementation, which produced NaN values that cascaded into the PCA step. Version 2 includes comprehensive numerical stability fixes and is now executing successfully.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original Combat implementation had multiple numerical stability issues:\n\n1. **Division by zero/NaN**: In the `_it_sol` function (line 25), the denominator `(t2 * n + d_old)` could become zero or NaN, causing invalid divide warnings and NaN propagation\n2. **Negative variance values**: The `delta_star` values could become negative, causing `sqrt()` operations to fail\n3. **Unchecked NaN propagation**: NaN values from the iterative solver propagated through to the final output matrix\n4. **Invalid t2 values**: The variance of gamma_hat (`t2`) could become negative or NaN, causing downstream failures\n\nThese numerical issues resulted in `X_corrected` containing NaN values, which then caused PCA to fail with:\n```\nValueError: Input X contains NaN.\nPCA does not accept missing values\
    \ encoded as NaN natively.\n```\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Version 2 Changes\nApplied comprehensive numerical stability improvements throughout the Combat implementation:\n\n1. **Safe division in `_it_sol` function**:\n   - Added explicit check for zero/NaN denominators before division\n   - Replace invalid denominators with small epsilon value (1e-8)\n   - Replace any resulting NaN values in `g_new` with previous `g_old` values\n\n2. **Variance protection**:\n   - Ensure `var_pooled` is always positive using `np.maximum(var_pooled, 1e-8)`\n   - Protect `delta_star` values before sqrt operation: `np.sqrt(np.maximum(delta_star[:, j], 1e-8))`\n   - Check for negative or NaN values in `d_new` and replace with `d_old`\n\n3. **t2 stabilization**:\n   - Added explicit check: `t2 = np.where(np.isnan(t2) | (t2 < 0), 1e-8, t2)`\n   - Prevents negative variance values from propagating\n\n4. **Final NaN cleanup**:\n   - Added fallback: `bayesdata = np.where(np.isnan(bayesdata), data, bayesdata)`\n\
    \   - Ensures no NaN values escape the Combat function\n   - Added explicit NaN check before PCA with warning message and replacement\n\n5. **Improved numerical precision**:\n   - Changed from `g_old + 1e-8` to `np.abs(g_old) + 1e-8` in convergence checks\n   - Prevents issues with negative values in relative error calculations\n\n### Key Code Sections Modified\n\n**In `_it_sol` function** (lines 24-34 in v2):\n```python\n# Add numerical stability: ensure denominators are never zero or NaN\ndenom = t2 * n + d_old\ndenom = np.where(np.isnan(denom) | (denom == 0), 1e-8, denom)\ng_new = (t2 * n * g_hat + d_old * g_bar) / denom\n\n# Replace any NaNs in g_new with g_old values\ng_new = np.where(np.isnan(g_new), g_old, g_new)\n```\n\n**In `run_combat` function** (lines 67-69, 76-78, 90-93 in v2):\n```python\n# Ensure positive variance\nvar_pooled = np.maximum(var_pooled, 1e-8)\n\n# Ensure t2 doesn't have extreme values\nt2 = np.where(np.isnan(t2) | (t2 < 0), 1e-8, t2)\n\n# Ensure positive\
    \ values before sqrt\ndsq = np.sqrt(np.maximum(delta_star[:, j], 1e-8)).reshape(-1, 1)\n```\n\n## Verification\nThe fix was verified using the `monitor_evaluation.py` script, which confirmed:\n- Exit code: 0 (SUCCESS)\n- Code ran for 300+ seconds without crashing\n- No Python exceptions or errors during execution\n- The evaluation is completing normally (just taking time due to computation)\n\n## Technical Notes\n- The Combat algorithm is inherently sensitive to numerical issues when dealing with batch correction\n- The original baseline implementation lacked sufficient safeguards for edge cases\n- The fixes maintain the algorithmic correctness while adding robustness\n- All fixes use standard practices: epsilon additions, explicit checks, and fallback values\n- The two-stage approach (Combat + Centroid Alignment) is now executing as intended\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update\
    \ them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n# --- Full Combat Baseline Code with Numerical Stability Fixes ---\ndef _aprior(delta_hat):\n    m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n    s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n    if s2 == 0: s2 = 1e-8\n    return (2 * s2 + m**2) / s2\n\ndef _bprior(delta_hat):\n    m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n    s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n    if s2 == 0: s2 = 1e-8\n    return (m * s2 + m**3) / s2\n\ndef _it_sol(s_data, g_hat, d_hat, g_bar, t2, a, b, conv=0.0001):\n    n = (~np.isnan(s_data)).sum(axis=1)\n    g_old, d_old = g_hat.copy(), d_hat.copy()\n    change, count = 1, 0\n    while change > conv and count < 100:\n        # Add numerical stability: ensure denominators are never zero or NaN\n        denom = t2 * n + d_old\n        denom = np.where(np.isnan(denom) | (denom == 0), 1e-8, denom)\n        g_new = (t2 * n * g_hat + d_old * g_bar) / denom\n\n        # Replace\
    \ any NaNs in g_new with g_old values\n        g_new = np.where(np.isnan(g_new), g_old, g_new)\n\n        sum2 = ((s_data - g_new.reshape((g_new.shape[0], 1))) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\n        d_new = (0.5 * sum2 + b) / (n / 2.0 + a - 1.0)\n\n        # Replace any NaNs or invalid values in d_new\n        d_new = np.where(np.isnan(d_new) | (d_new <= 0), d_old, d_new)\n\n        change = max((np.abs(g_new - g_old) / (np.abs(g_old) + 1e-8)).max(),\n                     (np.abs(d_new - d_old) / (np.abs(d_old) + 1e-8)).max())\n        g_old, d_old = g_new, d_new\n        count += 1\n    return g_new, d_new\n\ndef run_combat(adata: ad.AnnData, batch_key: str):\n    if issparse(adata.X): data = adata.X.toarray()\n    else: data = adata.X.copy()\n    data = data.T\n    batch_labels = adata.obs[batch_key].values\n    unique_batches = np.unique(batch_labels)\n    n_batches_total = len(unique_batches)\n    n_genes, n_samples = data.shape\n    batch_design = np.zeros((n_samples, n_batches_total))\n  \
    \  batch_info = [np.where(batch_labels == b)[0] for b in unique_batches]\n    n_batches = np.array([len(i) for i in batch_info])\n    for i, idx in enumerate(batch_info): batch_design[idx, i] = 1\n    B_hat = np.linalg.inv(batch_design.T @ batch_design) @ batch_design.T\n    grand_mean = np.mean(data, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    batch_means = (B_hat @ data.T).T\n    var_pooled = np.sum([np.sum((data[:, i] - batch_means[:, j:j+1])**2, axis=1) for j, i in enumerate(batch_info)], axis=0) / (n_samples - n_batches_total)\n    var_pooled = np.maximum(var_pooled, 1e-8)  # Ensure positive variance\n    stand_mean = grand_mean @ np.ones((1, n_samples))\n    s_data = np.zeros_like(data)\n    for j, i in enumerate(batch_info):\n        s_data[:, i] = (data[:, i] - batch_means[:, j:j+1]) / np.sqrt(var_pooled[:, np.newaxis] + 1e-8)\n\n    gamma_hat = (np.linalg.inv(batch_design.T @ batch_design) @ (batch_design.T @ s_data.T)).T\n    delta_hat = [np.var(s_data[:, i], axis=1, ddof=1) for i in batch_info]\n\
    \    gamma_bar = np.mean(gamma_hat, axis=1)\n    t2 = np.var(gamma_hat, axis=1, ddof=1)\n\n    # Ensure t2 doesn't have extreme values\n    t2 = np.where(np.isnan(t2) | (t2 < 0), 1e-8, t2)\n\n    a_prior = [_aprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n    b_prior = [_bprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n    gamma_star, delta_star = [], []\n    for j, i in enumerate(batch_info):\n        gamma, delta = _it_sol(s_data[:, i], gamma_hat[:, j], delta_hat[j], gamma_bar, t2, a_prior[j], b_prior[j])\n        gamma_star.append(gamma); delta_star.append(delta)\n    gamma_star, delta_star = np.array(gamma_star).T, np.array(delta_star).T\n\n    bayesdata = s_data.copy()\n    for j, i in enumerate(batch_info):\n        dsq = np.sqrt(np.maximum(delta_star[:, j], 1e-8)).reshape(-1, 1)  # Ensure positive values\n        denom = dsq @ np.ones((1, n_batches[j]))\n        numer = bayesdata[:, i] - (gamma_star[:, j].reshape(-1, 1) @ np.ones((1, n_batches[j])))\n        bayesdata[:, i] = numer / (denom + 1e-8)\n\n    vpsq\
    \ = np.sqrt(var_pooled).reshape(-1, 1)\n    bayesdata = bayesdata * (vpsq @ np.ones((1, n_samples))) + stand_mean\n\n    # Final check: replace any remaining NaNs with original data\n    bayesdata = np.where(np.isnan(bayesdata), data, bayesdata)\n\n    return bayesdata.T\n\n# --- Main Experiment Logic ---\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    print(\"--- Running Combat + Latent Space Centroid Alignment ---\")\n\n    # Stage 1: Standard Combat\n    adata_combat = adata.copy()\n    sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_combat, target_sum=1e4)\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata_combat)\n    X_corrected = run_combat(adata_combat, batch_key='batch')\n\n    # Check for NaNs before PCA\n    if np.any(np.isnan(X_corrected)):\n        print(f\"Warning: Found {np.sum(np.isnan(X_corrected))} NaN values after Combat, replacing with zeros\")\n        X_corrected = np.nan_to_num(X_corrected, nan=0.0)\n\n    # Stage 2: Centroid Alignment in PCA space\n    n_pcs = 50\n    X_emb = sc.pp.pca(X_corrected,\
    \ n_comps=n_pcs, svd_solver='arpack')\n\n    batch_labels = adata.obs['batch'].values\n    unique_batches = adata.obs['batch'].cat.categories\n\n    batch_centroids = {b: np.mean(X_emb[batch_labels == b, :], axis=0) for b in unique_batches}\n    global_centroid = np.mean(X_emb, axis=0)\n\n    X_emb_aligned = np.zeros_like(X_emb)\n    for batch in unique_batches:\n        mask = (batch_labels == batch)\n        X_emb_aligned[mask, :] = X_emb[mask, :] - batch_centroids[batch] + global_centroid\n\n    adata.obsm['X_emb'] = X_emb_aligned\n    return adata\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n--- Running Combat + Latent Space Centroid Alignment ---\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to:\
    \ storage/shared/tmp/c8374e42-112a-4f6d-9ce5-087548ab6139/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: c8374e42-112a-4f6d-9ce5-087548ab6139\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember to print detailed logs in your experiments; the STDOUT will be provided in the evaluation result. A well-designed metric can provide much richer insights than the score alone.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage write axiom/batch_robust_combat.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File created successfully: axiom/batch_robust_combat.py\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title\
    \ | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1092 | 1 | MNN Correction with Gaussian Kernel Smoothing | Axiom I | 710 | 0.5769 | 0.687 | 0.561 | 0.236 | 0.375 | 0.874 | 0.724 | 0.234 | 0.991 | 0.173 | 0.915 |\n| 1091 | 1 | Hybrid v4: Latent Standardization + MNN Residual Correction | Axiom I | 709 | 0.4697 | 0.411 | 0.538 | 0.216 | 0.368 | 0.830 | 0.629 | 0.000 | 0.990 | 0.000 | 0.716 |\n| 1089 | 1 | MNN-based Correction in Latent Space | Axiom I | 707 | 0.5728 | 0.757 | 0.450 | 0.224 | 0.366 | 0.860 | 0.702 | 0.277 | 0.993 | 0.277 | 0.823 |\n| 1087 | 1 | Hybrid v3: Latent Standardization + BBKNN Graph | Axiom I | 706 | 0.1759 | 0.117 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.210 | 0.431 | 0.000 | 1.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 |\n| 1085 | 1 | Latent Space Standardization (Mean and Variance Correction) | Axiom I | 705 | 0.6173 | 0.589 | 0.648 | 0.220 | 0.368\
