tick: 868
role: model
parts:
- text: "My second experiment, \"Combat + Centroid Alignment\" (ID 1223), has completed. The result is a score of 0.5889, which is effectively identical to the Combat baseline score of 0.5896. This is also a negative/neutral result.\n\n**Analysis of the Result:**\n\n*   **Debugger Report:** The debugger was again invoked, this time fixing numerical stability issues within the baseline Combat code itself. This is a very useful outcome, as I now have a more robust version of the baseline Combat implementation. The debugger's notes are invaluable, and I will incorporate these stability fixes into my stored `combat_baseline.py` module for future use.\n*   **Metric Breakdown:** The individual metrics are almost identical to the baseline. This indicates that the post-processing step of aligning centroids in PCA space had no significant positive or negative effect on the final outcome. The initial Combat correction was the dominant factor, and the simple geometric shift of centroids did not provide\
    \ any additional value.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nIdea 2 (\"Combat + Centroid Alignment\") is not a promising direction. The hypothesis that a simple geometric post-correction would improve the linear Combat result is falsified. This is a valuable negative result, as it suggests that any successful non-linear improvement will need to be more sophisticated than simple centroid alignment.\n\nI have now tested two of my three ideas, with one negative and one neutral result. I will now proceed to the final experiment in this phase of the Stagnation Protocol: testing Idea 3, **\"Batch-Robust Feature Selection for Combat.\"** I have already prepared the conceptual code. I will now create the final submission script, incorporating the newly debugger-hardened Combat implementation.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol 4b.3: Batch-Robust Feature Selection for Combat\"\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, combat, feature-selection, hvg\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests Idea\
    \ 3. It hypothesizes that Combat's performance can be improved by running it on a more robust set of genes. Highly variable genes (HVGs) are selected for each batch independently. The intersection of these gene sets is then used as the feature space for the standard Combat algorithm. This tests whether a consensus set of biologically varying genes provides a better substrate for correction.\"\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n  from functools import reduce\n\n  # --- Full, DEBUGGER-HARDENED Combat Baseline Code ---\n  def _aprior(delta_hat):\n      m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n      s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n      if s2 == 0: s2 = 1e-8\n      return (2 * s2 + m**2) / s2\n\n  def _bprior(delta_hat):\n      m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n      s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n      if s2 == 0: s2 = 1e-8\n      return (m * s2 + m**3) / s2\n\n  def _it_sol(s_data, g_hat, d_hat, g_bar, t2, a, b, conv=0.0001):\n      n = (~np.isnan(s_data)).sum(axis=1)\n\
    \      g_old, d_old = g_hat.copy(), d_hat.copy()\n      change, count = 1, 0\n      while change > conv and count < 100:\n          denom = t2 * n + d_old\n          denom = np.where(np.isnan(denom) | (denom == 0), 1e-8, denom)\n          g_new = (t2 * n * g_hat + d_old * g_bar) / denom\n          g_new = np.where(np.isnan(g_new), g_old, g_new)\n          sum2 = ((s_data - g_new.reshape((g_new.shape[0], 1))) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\n          d_new = (0.5 * sum2 + b) / (n / 2.0 + a - 1.0)\n          d_new = np.where(np.isnan(d_new) | (d_new <= 0), d_old, d_new)\n          change = max((np.abs(g_new - g_old) / (np.abs(g_old) + 1e-8)).max(), (np.abs(d_new - d_old) / (np.abs(d_old) + 1e-8)).max())\n          g_old, d_old = g_new, d_new\n          count += 1\n      return g_new, d_new\n\n  def run_combat(adata: ad.AnnData, batch_key: str):\n      if issparse(adata.X): data = adata.X.toarray()\n      else: data = adata.X.copy()\n      data = data.T\n      batch_labels = adata.obs[batch_key].values\n\
    \      unique_batches = np.unique(batch_labels)\n      batch_info = [np.where(batch_labels == b)[0] for b in unique_batches]\n      n_batches = np.array([len(i) for i in batch_info])\n      n_batches_total = len(unique_batches)\n      n_genes, n_samples = data.shape\n      batch_design = np.zeros((n_samples, n_batches_total))\n      for i, idx in enumerate(batch_info): batch_design[idx, i] = 1\n      B_hat = np.linalg.inv(batch_design.T @ batch_design) @ batch_design.T\n      grand_mean = np.mean(data, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      batch_means = (B_hat @ data.T).T\n      var_pooled = np.sum([np.sum((data[:, i] - batch_means[:, j:j+1])**2, axis=1) for j, i in enumerate(batch_info)], axis=0) / (n_samples - n_batches_total)\n      var_pooled = np.maximum(var_pooled, 1e-8)\n      stand_mean = grand_mean @ np.ones((1, n_samples))\n      s_data = np.zeros_like(data)\n      for j, i in enumerate(batch_info): s_data[:, i] = (data[:, i] - batch_means[:, j:j+1]) / np.sqrt(var_pooled[:, np.newaxis]\
    \ + 1e-8)\n      gamma_hat = (np.linalg.inv(batch_design.T @ batch_design) @ (batch_design.T @ s_data.T)).T\n      delta_hat = [np.var(s_data[:, i], axis=1, ddof=1) for i in batch_info]\n      gamma_bar = np.mean(gamma_hat, axis=1)\n      t2 = np.var(gamma_hat, axis=1, ddof=1)\n      t2 = np.where(np.isnan(t2) | (t2 < 0), 1e-8, t2)\n      a_prior = [_aprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n      b_prior = [_bprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n      gamma_star, delta_star = [], []\n      for j, i in enumerate(batch_info):\n          gamma, delta = _it_sol(s_data[:, i], gamma_hat[:, j], delta_hat[j], gamma_bar, t2, a_prior[j], b_prior[j])\n          gamma_star.append(gamma); delta_star.append(delta)\n      gamma_star, delta_star = np.array(gamma_star).T, np.array(delta_star).T\n      bayesdata = s_data.copy()\n      for j, i in enumerate(batch_info):\n          dsq = np.sqrt(np.maximum(delta_star[:, j], 1e-8)).reshape(-1, 1)\n          denom = dsq @ np.ones((1, n_batches[j]))\n          numer = bayesdata[:,\
    \ i] - (gamma_star[:, j].reshape(-1, 1) @ np.ones((1, n_batches[j])))\n          bayesdata[:, i] = numer / (denom + 1e-8)\n      vpsq = np.sqrt(var_pooled).reshape(-1, 1)\n      bayesdata = bayesdata * (vpsq @ np.ones((1, n_samples))) + stand_mean\n      bayesdata = np.where(np.isnan(bayesdata), data, bayesdata)\n      return bayesdata.T\n\n  # --- Main Experiment Logic ---\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"--- Running Batch-Robust Feature Selection for Combat ---\")\n      \n      # Stage 1: Identify Batch-Robust Genes\n      adata_hvg = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_hvg, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_hvg)\n      hvg_sets = []\n      batches = adata_hvg.obs['batch'].cat.categories\n      for batch in batches:\n          adata_batch = adata_hvg[adata_hvg.obs['batch'] == batch].copy()\n          sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_batch, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='cell_ranger')\n          hvg_sets.append(set(adata_batch.var_names[adata_batch.var['highly_variable']]))\n\
    \      \n      robust_hvgs = list(reduce(set.intersection, hvg_sets))\n      print(f\"Found {len(robust_hvgs)} 'batch-robust' genes.\")\n      \n      if len(robust_hvgs) < 50:\n          print(\"Warning: Very few common HVGs found. Falling back to standard HVG selection.\")\n          sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata_hvg, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3')\n          robust_hvgs = list(adata_hvg.var_names[adata_hvg.var['highly_variable']])\n\n      # Stage 2: Run Combat on the robust gene set\n      adata_robust = adata[:, robust_hvgs].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_robust, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_robust)\n      X_corrected_robust = run_combat(adata_robust, batch_key='batch')\n      \n      # Final PCA for embedding\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = sc.pp.pca(X_corrected_robust, n_comps=50, svd_solver='arpack')\n      return adata\n```"
