tick: 874
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter. The Stagnation Protocol has concluded for me, and I have a clear mandate to return to my primary research objective: solving the \"mixing shortfall\" in the SOTA pipeline.\n\nMy reflection session yielded a concrete plan. The first step is to test the \"Budgeted Mutualization\" hypothesis. This experiment is designed to verify that the catastrophic PCR=0 failure is caused by degree inflation, and that by strictly controlling node degrees during symmetrization, we can create a stable, non-collapsed graph.\n\nI will now implement this first hypothesis. The script will involve:\n1.  Importing the core SOTA pipeline logic from Praxis IV's modules.\n2.  Creating a new symmetrization function, `symmetrize_budgeted`, that implements the \"Budgeted Mutualization\" algorithm.\n3.  Modifying the `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` function to call my new symmetrizer instead of the default `_symmetrize_binary_with_distances`.\n4.  Running the full pipeline with\
    \ this modification.\n\nThis is a significant modification, so I will include the full, self-contained code in the submission for clarity and reproducibility.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Mixing Shortfall H1: Budgeted Mutualization Symmetrizer\"\ntags: \"sota, graph-theory, symmetrization, mixing-shortfall\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests my first hypothesis for solving the 'mixing shortfall' problem. It replaces the SOTA pipeline's default symmetrizer (A.maximum(A.T)) with a novel 'Budgeted Mutualization' algorithm. This new method preserves all mutual edges and then iteratively adds the shortest-distance one-way edges only if both nodes have a remaining degree budget (k_total). The primary goal is to prevent the degree inflation that is hypothesized to cause catastrophic graph collapse (PCR=0).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\
    \n  # --- Import core SOTA modules from Praxis storage ---\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  try:\n      from praxis_core import normalize_log1p_inplace, pca_array, one_hot_batches, ridge_batch_fit_predict\n  except ImportError:\n      raise ImportError(\"Could not import praxis_core module.\")\n\n  # --- My Novel Symmetrization Function ---\n  def symmetrize_budgeted(rows, cols, dists, n, k_total):\n      print(f\"--- Running Budgeted Mutualization (k_total={k_total}) ---\")\n      A_directed = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D_directed = sp.coo_matrix((dists, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\n      # 1. Preserve all mutual edges\n      A_mutual = A_directed.multiply(A_directed.T)\n      \n      # 2. Identify one-way edges\n      A_oneway = A_directed - A_mutual\n      oneway_rows, oneway_cols = A_oneway.nonzero()\n      \n      # 3. Create a sorted list of one-way edges by distance\n      oneway_dists = np.array(D_directed[oneway_rows,\
    \ oneway_cols]).flatten()\n      sorted_indices = np.argsort(oneway_dists)\n      \n      # 4. Calculate degree budgets\n      initial_degrees = A_mutual.sum(axis=1).A.flatten()\n      budgets = np.full(n, k_total, dtype=int) - initial_degrees\n      \n      # 5. Iteratively add one-way edges if budget allows\n      new_rows, new_cols, new_dists = [], [], []\n      A_final = A_mutual.tolil()\n\n      print(f\"Starting with {A_mutual.nnz // 2} mutual edges. Processing {len(sorted_indices)} one-way edges...\")\n      for idx in sorted_indices:\n          u, v = oneway_rows[idx], oneway_cols[idx]\n          if budgets[u] > 0 and budgets[v] > 0:\n              if A_final[u, v] == 0: # Check if edge (or its symmetric) has not been added\n                  A_final[u, v] = 1\n                  A_final[v, u] = 1\n                  budgets[u] -= 1\n                  budgets[v] -= 1\n      \n      A_final = A_final.tocsr()\n      D_final = D_directed.maximum(D_directed.T) # Use simple max for\
    \ distances\n      A_final.eliminate_zeros()\n      D_final = D_final.multiply(A_final) # Keep only distances for existing edges\n      \n      print(f\"Finished. Final graph has {A_final.nnz // 2} symmetric edges.\")\n      return A_final, D_final\n\n  # --- Modified version of Praxis's BBSG to use my symmetrizer ---\n  def build_density_adaptive_bbsg_MODIFIED(Zcorr, batches, k_total, metric, delta, k_density, rng_seed):\n      # This is the same DAQB/BBSG logic from praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py\n      # The only change is the final call to the symmetrization function.\n      n = Zcorr.shape[0]\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B_other = max(len(cats) - 1, 1)\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Zcorr)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Zcorr)\n      d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = np.min(d_k), np.max(d_k)\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n      base_cross = (len(cats) - 1)\
    \ / float(len(cats))\n      mix_delta = (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * float(delta)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + mix_delta, 0.60, 0.90)\n      max_cross_per = int(np.ceil(0.9 * k_total / B_other))\n      max_within = int(np.ceil((1.0 - 0.60) * k_total))\n      batch_to_indices, nn_models = {}, {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == c)\n          batch_to_indices[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          nn_models[j] = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=max(max_within, max_cross_per) + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Zcorr[mask])\n      nn_dists, nn_idx_local = {}, {}\n      for j in range(len(cats)):\n          d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Zcorr)\n          nn_dists[j], nn_idx_local[j] = d, ii\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n      batch_codes = {val: idx for idx, val in enumerate(cats)}\n      bc = np.array([batch_codes[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = int(bc[i])\n          q_cross_total = int(round(f_cross[i]\
    \ * k_total))\n          q_within = max(0, min(k_total, int(round(k_total - q_cross_total))))\n          other_batches = [j for j in range(len(cats)) if j != bi]\n          d_i, ii = nn_dists[bi][i], nn_idx_local[bi][i]\n          start = 1 if d_i[0] == 0.0 else 0\n          sel = min(q_within, d_i.shape[0] - start)\n          if sel > 0:\n              cols.extend(batch_to_indices[bi][ii[start:start + sel]])\n              rows.extend([i] * sel)\n              dvals.extend(d_i[start:start + sel])\n          q_per_other = q_cross_total // B_other\n          rem = q_cross_total - q_per_other * B_other\n          batch_scores = sorted([(j, nn_dists[j][i][0]) for j in other_batches], key=lambda t: t[1])\n          q_map = {j: q_per_other for j in other_batches}\n          for k in range(rem): q_map[batch_scores[k % len(other_batches)][0]] += 1\n          for j in other_batches:\n              d_ij, ii_j = nn_dists[j][i], nn_idx_local[j][i]\n              sel_j = min(q_map[j], d_ij.shape[0])\n\
    \              if sel_j > 0:\n                  cols.extend(batch_to_indices[j][ii_j[:sel_j]])\n                  rows.extend([i] * sel_j)\n                  dvals.extend(d_ij[:sel_j])\n      # --- MODIFICATION POINT ---\n      return symmetrize_budgeted(np.asarray(rows), np.asarray(cols), np.asarray(dvals), n, k_total)\n\n  # --- Main SOTA pipeline function, modified to call the new graph builder ---\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Use SOTA parameters from Praxis IV\n      alpha_g, n_pcs_graph, lam_override = 0.5, 50, 0.605\n      k_total, kdensity, delta = 50, 22, 0.10\n      \n      normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32)\n\
    \      \n      # Dummy embedding path for compatibility\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = sc.pp.pca(Xd, n_comps=50)\n      \n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      Xw_graph = Xd * np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_g)\n      Zg = pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=1e-3)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(0.74, float(lam_override) * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      \n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg_MODIFIED(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches, k_total, 'cosine', delta, kdensity, 0)\n      \n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
