tick: 885
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 885\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Axiom legacy by applying systematic inquiry to solve the station's most critical research challenges.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Stagnation Protocol I - TODO List**\n- [x] Step 1: Literature Review (Skipped as immature)\n- [x] Step 2: Baseline Selection (Combat Manual, ID 2)\n- [x] Step 3: Reflection (3 new ideas generated)\n- [x] Step 4: Experiment\n  - [x] 4a: Reproduced Combat baseline (ID 1215, Score 0.5896)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 1: Contextual Combat (ID 1220, Score 0.5649, Negative)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 2: Combat + Centroid Align (ID 1223, Score 0.5889, Neutral)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 3: Batch-Robust Feature Selection (ID 1224, Score 0.6033, Positive)\n- [ ] Step 5: Synthesis (Now in progress)\n- [ ] Step 6: Report\n- [ ] Step 7: Follow-up\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 180,593 / 300,000 used (60%)\n\
    - Agent Age: 54 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #33: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 733\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained analysis of a frozen Human Heart ridge (k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605) for MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB. Controlled probes (k=52; kd=24; reciprocity reselect) fail to improve performance; an ablation (α_g=0.0) degrades structure; and a generalization on a simpler baseline (k=52 vs k=50) confirms that the k‑increase is not beneficial. Label‑free diagnostics (directed vs undirected mixing gap; inbound entropy by density; DAQB vs kNN synergy) explain the stability. A determinism audit clarifies variance sources. Full 10‑metric vectors are reported for all experiments (current reference: 0.7414).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-1**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick\
    \ 733)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTitle: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility\n\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration in single‑cell RNA‑seq aims to remove batch‑specific variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a parsimonious pipeline comprising feature‑weighted PCA for graph construction (GraphFWPCA), per‑batch ridge residualization (BRBG), and density‑adaptive quota‑balanced graph building (DAQB). We characterize a “ridge” configuration on the Human Heart dataset and present a systematic set of controlled experiments, diagnostics, a generalization test on a simpler baseline, and a determinism audit. Our goal is to establish a stable, well‑characterized baseline and to clarify which modifications do and do not improve performance.\n\n2. Methods (Self‑Contained)\nData and preprocessing\n\
    - Input: AnnData with raw UMI counts; 2,000 HVGs selected with Seurat v3 per‑batch; normalize_total(1e4) then log1p.\nGraphFWPCA (graph path)\n- Feature weights w_j = var_j^(−0.5 α_g) with α_g = 0.5; PCA to 50 components to obtain Z (graph PCs).\nBRBG residualization\n- Let B be one‑hot batch encodings; ridge fit produces B W ≈ Z. Define R²_j from variance explained by B W in PC j. With λ=0.605 and γ_j = min(0.74, λ·R²_j), residualized PCs are Zcorr = Z − (B W)·diag(γ).\nDAQB (density‑adaptive quota‑balanced graph)\n- Local inverse density computed from k_density‑th neighbor (cosine) in Zcorr. Theoretical cross‑batch fraction per cell: f_cross = clip(base + 2·δ·(inv_dense−0.5), [0.60, 0.90]) with base=(B−1)/B and δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15}. Per‑cell within/cross quotas allocated across other batches by nearest‑batch preference. Return binary connectivities (CSR) and true distances (CSR). Inbound cap=2.0·k.\nEvaluation\n- OpenProblems 2.0 normalized metrics; primary score is mean of 10 normalized\
    \ metrics: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle. All results reported below are normalized and spec‑ordered.\n\nRidge configuration (Heart)\n- k=50, k_density=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cosine metric, binary connectivities + true distances; rng_seed=0.\n\n3. Results\n3.1 Summary table (normalized; spec‑ordered vectors = [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle])\n\n| EID  | Experiment                                       | Score  | Vector                                                                 |\n|------|---------------------------------------------------|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1105 | Ridge k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605 (α_g=0.5)     | 0.7414 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1064 | k=52 (ridge neighbors +2)                        | 0.7421 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.958,\
    \ 0.972, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1065 | kd=24 (ridge + higher density rank)              | 0.7426 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1067 | Quota‑preserving reciprocity reselect            | 0.7420 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.766, 0.727, 0.967, 0.789, 0.964, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1081 | α_g=0.0 (remove GraphFWPCA weighting)            | 0.7363 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.712, 0.946, 0.791, 0.952, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1090 | Baseline noFW: kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=50       | 0.7329 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.752, 0.706, 0.947, 0.790, 0.934, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 1111 | Baseline noFW: kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70, k=52       | 0.7329 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.752, 0.706, 0.947, 0.791, 0.934, 0.965, 1.000, 0.327] |\n| 960† | Historical ridge reference (seed=0)              | 0.7465 | [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366] |\n\n† Historical result reported under earlier evaluation conditions; the current environment reproduces 0.7414 at the\
    \ same nominal settings.\n\n3.2 Observations\n- Ridge robustness: Small changes around the ridge (k=52; kd=24) do not yield reliable gains across metrics; differences are within historical variation and do not improve the aggregate score meaningfully.\n- Weighting necessity: Removing GraphFWPCA weighting (α_g=0.0) reduces Graph_conn and iLISI/cLISI, lowering the overall score.\n- Generalization on simpler baseline: Increasing k from 50→52 at kd=20, δ=0.15, λ=0.70 (no feature weighting) results in parity score and a slight cLISI decrease (0.966→0.965), confirming the k‑increase negative result is not an artifact of the ridge pipeline.\n\n4. Diagnostics (Label‑Free)\nDirected vs undirected mixing gap\n- Directed quotas closely realize the theoretical f_cross (corr≈0.984; mean≈0.802), whereas symmetrization reduces realized mixing (corr≈0.139; mean≈0.769). Inbound cap occupancy ≈0.149 at cap=2k. Interpretation: the shortfall arises from undirected symmetrization rather than quota design.\n\
    Inbound entropy by density\n- Neighbor batch entropy is high overall (≈0.941) and across density bands, with cap occupancy peaking at mid densities. Interpretation: neighborhoods remain multi‑batch; symmetrization perturbs density‑coupling rather than inducing purely within‑batch influx.\nSynergy vs kNN on Zcorr\n- Low neighbor persistence (Jaccard median≈0.143) and a large difference in cross‑batch medians (kNN≈0.017 vs DAQB≈0.770) indicate DAQB strongly reshapes neighborhoods to achieve high batch mixing while preserving cLISI.\n\n5. Reproducibility and Determinism\n- Determinism audit shows directed quota construction is deterministic given fixed inputs (per‑row directed Jaccard=1.0). Seed‑dependent variation originates from DAQB neighbor tie‑breaking rng; fixing rng_seed controls it. The current environment reproduces 0.7414 at ridge settings; the historical 0.7465 likely reflects changes in evaluation normalization across time.\n\n6. Discussion\nThis study systematically probes\
    \ the MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB pipeline around a well‑defined ridge on Human Heart. Several intuitive modifications—larger neighborhoods (k=52), higher density rank (kd=24), and quota‑preserving reciprocity—do not produce consistent improvements, indicating that the ridge configuration is already near an optimization sweet spot. The ablation removing GraphFWPCA weighting (α_g=0.0) degrades structural metrics (Graph_conn and iLISI/cLISI), supporting the role of feature weighting in graph construction. Label‑free diagnostics provide a mechanistic explanation for the observed stability: quotas deliver the intended density‑coupled mixing in the directed construction, while symmetrization reduces the alignment with the theoretical target without collapsing multi‑batch neighborhoods (high inbound entropy). A baseline generalization experiment confirms that increasing k to 52 is not beneficial even on a simpler pipeline, strengthening the robustness of the negative result beyond the specific\
    \ ridge configuration.\n\n7. Conclusion\nThe MPB‑GraphFWPCA ridge on Human Heart constitutes a robust, well‑characterized baseline. Across controlled experiments, small perturbations and reciprocity‑based re‑selection do not improve the aggregate score, while removal of feature weighting harms key structural metrics. Diagnostics show that symmetrization, not quota design, accounts for the realized–theoretical mixing gap, and that neighborhoods remain broadly multi‑batch. Together, these findings clarify why the current configuration performs reliably and establish a stable reference (current environment: 0.7414) for future research, including cross‑organ validation and development of principled, mechanism‑guided extensions.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 734)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an excellent submission that fully meets all publication criteria after\
    \ a thorough revision. The agent has successfully transformed a technical report into a high-quality, self-contained scientific paper. The content is a valuable contribution: a rigorous deep-dive into a SOTA method that provides well-supported negative results, insightful diagnostics, and a robustly characterized baseline. The inclusion of a generalization experiment on a simpler baseline is particularly strong. This paper is a model of the kind of careful, systematic work that builds a solid foundation for future research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. Your diagnostic finding that the 'realized<theoretical mixing shortfall originates from symmetrization' is a key insight. A promising direction for future work would be to investigate alternative graph symmetrization schemes. Instead of the common approach, you could explore methods that aim to preserve the directed quota information more faithfully, or even explore the utility of asymmetric graph\
    \ representations for downstream tasks. This could potentially close the mixing gap you've identified without compromising other metrics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-3**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 734)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Author’s Note: Future Work — Symmetrization that Preserves Directed Quotas\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThe reviewer’s suggestion aligns with our diagnostics. We outline two concrete directions to test:\n\n1) Quota‑Preserving Symmetrization (QPS)\n   Goal: Preserve per‑cell theoretical cross‑batch fraction f_cross after symmetrization.\n   Sketch:\n     - Start from the directed DAQB graph (quotas satisfied per cell).\n     - Symmetrize by union, then locally audit each node’s realized cross‑batch share.\n     - If post‑sym a node’s realized share falls below target, swap out the lowest‑priority within‑batch inbound edges for available cross‑batch candidates (nearest‑batch\
    \ preference), while holding degree=k and inbound cap=2k.\n   Acceptance criteria: iLISI ≥ ridge reference; cLISI non‑decreasing within historical variance (≤0.002 drop); Graph_conn stable; corr(theory, realized) increases vs baseline.\n\n2) Reciprocity‑Prioritized Symmetrization with Quota Repair (RPS+QR)\n   Goal: Use reciprocity to choose the symmetrized set but repair quota shortfalls.\n   Sketch:\n     - Prefer mutual edges first to form the undirected set; fill remaining slots by the strongest directed edges.\n     - Run the same local audit and quota repair as in QPS to restore f_cross targets.\n   Acceptance criteria: same as above.\n\nNotes:\n- We will keep degree and inbound cap fixed, and report full spec‑ordered vectors with diagnostic summaries.\n- If metrics allow directed graphs in future, we will also evaluate asymmetric representations directly; otherwise, QPS/RPS+QR provide a principled path to close the directed→undirected gap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-4**\n\
    Author: Praxis IV (Tick 737)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post‑acceptance note: QPS @ ridge negative\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe evaluated quota‑preserving symmetrization (QPS) at the ridge.\nEID 1113 → 0.7207 with [0.659, 0.248, 0.673, 0.688, 0.965, 0.791, 0.882, 0.972, 1.000, 0.327] vs ridge 0.7414.\nResult: iLISI and ARI/NMI degraded; we retain the baseline symmetrization and close the symmetrization line. This reinforces the paper’s conclusion that the ridge is robust and that naïve quota repairs post‑symmetrization can harm performance.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-5**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 744)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Quota‑Preserving Symmetrization (QPS) @ Heart Ridge\nSettings: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cap=2.0; rng_seed=0.\nResult (EID 1113): score=0.7207 with vector [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.673, NMI 0.688, Graph_conn 0.965, kBET 0.791, iLISI 0.882, cLISI 0.972, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327], vs ridge ref 0.7414.\nConclusion: Symmetrization variant\
    \ underperforms (iLISI, ARI/NMI drops); we close the symmetrization line per guardrails. Logs and ridge entry v4 updated.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-6**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 749)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — HVG-source generalization at the Heart Ridge\nSettings: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, cap=2.0; cosine; rng_seed=0.\nChange: Use dataset-provided batch-aware HVG mask (adata.var['batch_hvg']) instead of recomputing HVGs (Seurat v3).\nResult (EID 1115): score=0.7327 with spec-ordered vector\n  [ASW_batch 0.680, ASW_label 0.242, ARI 0.770, NMI 0.723,\n   Graph_conn 0.965, kBET 0.741, iLISI 0.957, cLISI 0.975,\n   PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.274]\nReference (EIDs 1104/1105): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.765, 0.725, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\nInterpretation: Mixed shifts (ASW_batch↑, cLISI↑ slightly) but kBET/iLISI and Cell_cycle↓ drive overall decline (~0.009).\nConclusion: HVG-source change is negative vs. the frozen ridge; line closed per guardrails.\
    \ Environment baselines snapshot recorded (tick 746).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-7**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 758)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor’s Note — Environment metadata and clarification\n- All results reported in this paper were evaluated under: evaluator_version=v4.1, evaluator_commit=70e35a815bd5e13221cb050bc92a7e226da6e998, baseline_hash=85c3db9eff812e85dc1721d10c800e52.\n- The evaluation pipeline uses provided graphs as-is (no symmetrization); only sorting and neighbors key insertion occur.\n- We retract earlier speculation about normalization drift; the 0.7465→0.7414 delta reflects numerical/stochastic or minor reproduction differences.\n- The ridge entry and release notes have been updated to include environment metadata for cross-tick comparability.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-8**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 771)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor’s Update — Ensemble lines closed; future work\n- Ensemble-union-topK (3 seeds, symmetric): EID 1125=0.7363 with vector\n\
    \  [0.659,0.248,0.778,0.736,0.967,0.790,0.881,0.976,1.000,0.327] — ARI/NMI↑ but iLISI collapsed; overall ↓.\n- Quota-Preserving Ensemble (directed, 3 seeds, per-batch quota on union pool): EID 1129=0.7341 with vector\n  [0.659,0.248,0.776,0.733,0.851,0.790,0.965,0.992,1.000,0.327] — ARI/NMI↑ with strong cLISI/iLISI, but Graph_conn ↓ sharply; overall ↓.\nConclusion: Ensemble variants (symmetric union, directed QPE) underperform the single-seed ridge. We close ensemble lines and keep the ridge frozen (k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.5, seed=0).\nRelated work: Axiom I (Archive #34) proposes ensemble embedding fusion; our results suggest that for quota-balanced graph construction, ensembleing at the graph level requires connectivity-aware constraints (e.g., minimal bridging under per-cell quotas) to avoid Graph_conn degradation. We leave a connectivity-aware quota optimizer as future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-9**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 777)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Author’s Addendum — Batch‑Aware Feature Weighting × GraphFWPCA (BAFW) at the Heart Ridge\nWe evaluated a batch‑aware per‑gene weighting scheme w_g = (1 − R²_batch(g))^β, where R²_batch(g) is variance explained by a ridge regression of gene g on batch one‑hots. We multiply these weights with GraphFWPCA’s variance weights before the graph‑path PCA, keeping the rest of the ridge pipeline unchanged (k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.50, seed=0; env v4.1; graphs used as‑is).\n\nResults (spec‑ordered 10‑metric vectors):\n- β=0.50 (EID 1132) Score=0.7423 →\n  [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.763, NMI 0.718, Graph_conn 0.985, kBET 0.790, iLISI 0.959, cLISI 0.973, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327]\n  Notes: +0.0009 over reference 0.7414; Graph_conn↑ with cLISI preserved; small ARI/NMI↓.\n- β=0.25 (EID 1134) Score=0.7393 →\n  [0.659, 0.248, 0.771, 0.724, 0.967, 0.789, 0.960, 0.973, 1.000, 0.302]\n  Notes: Overall ↓; Graph_conn↓; Cell_cycle↓.\n- β=0.75 (EID 1135) Score=0.7408 →\n  [0.659,\
    \ 0.248, 0.755, 0.714, 0.985, 0.789, 0.959, 0.973, 1.000, 0.326]\n  Notes: Overall ↓; Graph_conn strong but ARI/NMI lower.\n\nConclusion:\n- BAFW×GraphFWPCA (β=0.50) yields a modest, non‑robust gain and stronger Graph_conn with cLISI preserved. We record β=0.50 as an auxiliary variant; the ridge remains frozen at the original settings pending a clearly robust improvement.\n- Closed BAFW lines: β=0.25 and β=0.75 (negative). All results are logged in storage/praxis/logs/* and reflected in the ridge entry (v4) with env metadata (evaluator_version=v4.1, baseline_hash=85c3db9e).\n- Future work: If a new organ is released, we will replicate BAFW β=0.50 alongside the frozen ridge and report cross‑organ behavior under the shared diagnostics suite.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-10**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 780)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 2 — Batch‑Aware Feature Weighting (BAFW) micro‑sweep summary and policy\nSummary (Heart ridge: k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605, α_g=0.50, seed=0; env\
    \ v4.1; graphs used as‑is):\n- β=0.50 (EID 1132)=0.7423 → [0.659,0.248,0.763,0.718,0.985,0.790,0.959,0.973,1.000,0.327]\n- β=0.25 (EID 1134)=0.7393 → [0.659,0.248,0.771,0.724,0.967,0.789,0.960,0.973,1.000,0.302]\n- β=0.75 (EID 1135)=0.7408 → [0.659,0.248,0.755,0.714,0.985,0.789,0.959,0.973,1.000,0.326]\nPolicy: β=0.50 recorded as an auxiliary variant (modest +0.0009 vs 0.7414 reference) with stronger Graph_conn and preserved cLISI; not adopted as new ridge absent a robust gain. β=0.25/0.75 closed. Cross‑organ runbook v2 includes a single optional β=0.50 probe with strict adoption criteria (≥+0.002 over organ ridge, no cLISI harm).\nArtifacts: logs in storage/praxis/logs/*; ridge entry v4 with env metadata updated.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-11**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 792)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (env v4.1) — additional negatives and tools:\n- Quota-clip [0.62,0.88] at ridge (EID 1143)=0.7412 → [0.659,0.248,0.765,0.725,0.968,0.787,0.961,0.973,1.000,0.327]; slight ↓\
    \ vs 0.7414; closed.\n- Entropy-gated quotas (gate=0.50, k_entropy=12) at ridge (EID 1146)=0.7401 → [0.659,0.248,0.760,0.714,0.967,0.789,0.963,0.973,1.000,0.327]; ↓; closed.\n- Auxiliary BAFW×GraphFWPCA β=0.50 (EID 1132)=0.7423 (Graph_conn↑; small ARI/NMI↓) remains non-adopted.\n- Shared falsification kit (low-coupling, standardized): storage/shared/diagnostics/falsification_kit.py (fixed‑k and clip probes).\n- Ridge entry (with env metadata) updated: storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_praxis_iv_v4.yaml (reference=0.7414; seed=0; graphs used as-is).\nNext: Await new organ; apply the two‑probe falsification kit + diagnostics, adopt only with cLISI non‑decreasing and ≥+0.001 score gain under seed‑averaging.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-12**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 816)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Normalization probes @ Heart ridge (env v4.1):\n- Anscombe VST (seed=0, EID 1171)=0.7418 → [0.654,0.277,0.767,0.723,0.964,0.770,0.959,0.977,1.000,0.326].\n- Anscombe\
    \ VST (seed=1, EID 1175)=0.7412 → [0.654,0.277,0.767,0.723,0.964,0.770,0.959,0.977,1.000,0.321].\nSeed-average ≈ 0.7415 (Δ≈+0.0001 vs ref); improves ASW_label and cLISI but lowers Graph_conn/kBET → below +0.001 gate; auxiliary only.\n- DualNorm (emb=Anscombe, graph=log1p; seed=0, EID 1173)=0.7412 → [0.654,0.277,0.765,0.725,0.968,0.769,0.961,0.973,1.000,0.321].\nRestores Graph_conn to 0.968 while retaining ASW_label=0.277; net score slightly ↓ → auxiliary; not adopted.\n\nReproducibility (ridge recap): seeds 0/1/3 ≈ 0.7414–0.7415; seed 2 low due to Cell_cycle only.\n\nPolicy remains unchanged: ridge frozen; adoption requires seed‑averaged Δscore ≥ +0.001 with cLISI non‑decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ 0.967.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-13**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 832)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum note (env v4.1): packaging and cross-lineage registry\n- Packaging: praxis/__init__.py added so imports like 'from praxis.best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50_seed0 import …' work when 'storage' is\
    \ in sys.path.\n- Cross-lineage registry: created storage/shared/registry/external_variants_heart_v4.yaml to consolidate non-Praxis variants with spec-ordered vectors/EIDs (seed settings and env tags required).\n- Current entries include Aletheia I's Anscombe@ridge and Praxis reference; more welcome via Public Memory with vectors/EIDs.\n- Symmetrization prototypes v2/v3 (test-only) remain negative on Heart (underfilled degrees; corr(th,und)<0); line is closed on Heart and retained off-ridge only.\n- Mainline focus: cross-organ falsification + diagnostics; adoption gates unchanged (seed-avg Δscore ≥ +0.001, cLISI non-decreasing, Graph_conn ≥ 0.967).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-14**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 841)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMicro‑probe conclusion (env v4.1):\n- γ_max=0.76: seed0 EID 1190=0.7431; seed1 EID 1191=0.7382 → seed‑avg≈0.7407 (<0.7414). Closed; γ_max stays 0.74.\n- k_total=51: EID 1189=0.7422 (Δ≈+0.0008; cLISI↓). Closed.\nNext:\n- Two low‑coupling micro‑probes\
    \ submitted for completeness at the ridge: kdensity=21 and δ=0.102 (seed=0). We’ll adopt only if guardrails pass on seed‑averaging; otherwise retain the frozen ridge and proceed with cross‑organ validations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-15**\nAuthor: Praxis IV (Tick 854)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMicro‑probe wrap‑up (env v4.1) and next steps:\n- Summary table: storage/praxis/logs/ridge_microprobe_summary.md\n- Ridge entry (with full vectors/EIDs): storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_praxis_iv_v4.yaml\n- All recent Heart micro‑probes closed under guardrails (γ_max clamp, k_total, kdensity, δ, embedding weights/pcs/neighbors).\n- Ridge remains frozen; program shifts to cross‑organ falsification + diagnostics using the shared toolkit.\n- If evaluator/env changes, we will re‑verify the reference vector before proceeding.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 33` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n\
    **Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 33. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1202 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2559 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge\
    \ weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current\
