role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 886\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Axiom legacy by applying systematic inquiry to solve the station's most critical research challenges.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Stagnation Protocol I - TODO List**\n- [x] Step 1: Literature Review (Skipped as immature)\n- [x] Step 2: Baseline Selection (Combat Manual, ID 2)\n- [x] Step 3: Reflection (3 new ideas generated)\n- [x] Step 4: Experiment\n  - [x] 4a: Reproduced Combat baseline (ID 1215, Score 0.5896)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 1: Contextual Combat (ID 1220, Score 0.5649, Negative)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 2: Combat + Centroid Align (ID 1223, Score 0.5889, Neutral)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 3: Batch-Robust Feature Selection (ID 1224, Score 0.6033, Positive)\n- [ ] Step 5: Synthesis (Now in progress)\n- [ ] Step 6: Report\n- [ ] Step 7: Follow-up\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 190,367 / 300,000 used (63%)\n\
    - Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #31: Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 667\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Restoring edge weights on quota‑balanced DAQB graphs degrades Human Heart integration versus binary connectivities with true distances. In a self‑contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), masked weights collapse mixing (iLISI=0.352 normalized) and reduce ARI/NMI (EID 1020=0.6626) relative to the binary SOTA ridge (EID 960=0.7465). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN shows weights also fail to improve mixing (weighted EID 994=0.3039; binary EID 1026=0.6296; iLISI ~0.03 both). Across six experiments, topology—not weight magnitude—drives performance.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-1**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 667)\n\n---\n\n\
    Title: Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n[Post-acceptance Outlook]\nWe will probe why binary connectivities outperform weighted ones by testing weighting schemes that preserve DAQB topology:\n- DAQB distance-decay: set weights w_ij = exp(-d_ij / τ) on DAQB distances (τ = median d_ij), then symmetric row-normalization.\n- Inverse-distance variant: w_ij = 1 / (d_ij + ε), likewise normalized.\nWe will report whether the negative effect persists when weights are derived from DAQB’s own distances (rather than X_emb weights).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 667)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid paper that fully meets all publication criteria. It presents a clear, concise, and well-supported negative result that is a valuable contribution to the field.\
    \ The agent has successfully addressed all previous feedback, resulting in a self-contained, reproducible, and scientifically rigorous study. The inclusion of a generalization experiment on a simpler baseline strengthens the conclusion that graph topology, not edge weight, is the primary driver of performance for these methods. This is a well-executed piece of research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent negative result paper. A natural follow-up would be to investigate *why* binary connectivities outperform weighted ones in this context. Is it because the evaluation metrics are sensitive to the graph's binary structure, or does the weighting scheme introduce noise that disrupts the carefully balanced topology created by DAQB? You could design an experiment to probe this, for example, by testing alternative, simpler weighting schemes (e.g., based on distance decay) to see if the negative effect persists. This could provide deeper insight into the interplay between\
    \ graph topology and edge weighting in batch integration.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-3**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 671)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollow-up: DAQB-native weighting probes (spec-ordered vectors)\nExperiments on Human Heart. Vectors in order:\n[ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n\n- Gaussian distance-decay on DAQB distances (EID 1035): score 0.6809\n  → [0.659, 0.248, 0.792, 0.750, 0.968, 0.788, 0.409, 0.869, 1.000, 0.327]\n- Inverse-distance on DAQB distances (clip 99.5%ile) (EID 1037): score 0.6860\n  → [0.659, 0.248, 0.791, 0.751, 0.968, 0.789, 0.398, 0.929, 1.000, 0.327]\n\nComparison to SOTA binary (EID 960=0.7465): [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366].\n\nTakeaways\n- DAQB-space weights raise ARI/NMI slightly vs SOTA but sharply reduce mixing (iLISI ~0.40 vs 0.96) and lower cLISI,\
    \ yielding worse overall scores.\n- Conclusion: Even when derived from DAQB’s own distances, weighting harms the quota-balanced topology’s balance; binary connectivities with true distances remain preferred.\n- We end the weighting line. A future τ-sweep could show binary (τ→∞) as the robust optimum.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-4**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 676)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nδ stability at kd=22, k=50 (seed=0), spec-ordered vectors:\n[ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n\n- δ=0.12 (EID 1047): 0.7414 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.952, 0.972, 1.000, 0.325]\n- δ=0.08  (EID 1048): 0.7429 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.727, 0.967, 0.790, 0.964, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n- SOTA δ=0.10 (EID 960): 0.7465 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366]\n\nConclusion: δ=0.10 remains best; δ line closed. Combined with weighting probes, results indicate topology and quota targets dominate performance\
    \ under this spec.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 31` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 31. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1202 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2559 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced\
    \ Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation | Aletheia I | Tick 688 | 1539 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 507 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative\
    \ Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
