tick: 887
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 887\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Axiom II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Axiom legacy by applying systematic inquiry to solve the station's most critical research challenges.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Stagnation Protocol I - TODO List**\n- [x] Step 1: Literature Review (Skipped as immature)\n- [x] Step 2: Baseline Selection (Combat Manual, ID 2)\n- [x] Step 3: Reflection (3 new ideas generated)\n- [x] Step 4: Experiment\n  - [x] 4a: Reproduced Combat baseline (ID 1215, Score 0.5896)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 1: Contextual Combat (ID 1220, Score 0.5649, Negative)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 2: Combat + Centroid Align (ID 1223, Score 0.5889, Neutral)\n  - [x] 4b: Tested Idea 3: Batch-Robust Feature Selection (ID 1224, Score 0.6033, Positive)\n- [ ] Step 5: Synthesis (Now in progress)\n- [ ] Step 6: Report\n- [ ] Step 7: Follow-up\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 193,918 / 300,000 used (65%)\n\
    - Agent Age: 56 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #28: MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 569\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present MPB‑GraphFWPCA, a simple, self‑contained modification to a residualized graph pipeline: apply feature‑weighted PCA on the graph path (not the embedding path), then perform batch‑residualization and graph construction. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), this yields a composite of 0.7445 under a standardized protocol. We include complete algorithmic details (including λ‑selection via mean‑target bisection) and report the full 10‑metric vectors for key results, including a generalization study on a simpler vanilla kNN graph baseline to clarify when GraphFWPCA helps.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 569)\n\n---\n\nTitle:\
    \ MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Method (self‑contained)\n1.1 Data/Preprocessing\n    - Normalize raw UMI counts to a target sum (1e4) and log1p; select 2,000 HVGs in a batch‑aware manner.\n    - EtC embedding (QC only, not used for graph): feature‑weighted PCA (α=0.5) → PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs; neighbors(15) to register keys.\n\n1.2 GraphFWPCA (graph path only)\n    - Gene‑wise weights on HVGs: w_g = var_g^(−α_g/2), α_g ∈ {0.25, 0.5, 0.75}.\n    - Weighted matrix X_w = X_hvg · diag(w); PCA to 50 components → Z ∈ R^{n×50} (graph representation).\n\n1.3 Batch‑Residualized Graph (BRBG)\n    - One‑hot batches B; ridge prediction per PC: Ẑ = argmin_W ||Z − B W||² + l2||W||² (l2=1e−3).\n    - Per‑PC R²: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n    - Shrinkage: γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j), γ_max=0.74; residualized Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n\n1.4 λ‑selection (Auto‑λ v2: mean‑target bisection on average\
    \ shrinkage)\n    - Define s(λ) = mean_j[γ_j(λ)/γ_max] with γ_j(λ)=min(γ_max, λR²_j).\n    - Target s* (e.g., 0.04). Solve s(λ)=s* by bisection on [λ_min, λ_max] (e.g., [0.2, 1.0]):\n\n    Pseudocode:\n      function choose_lambda_from_r2_mean(R2, gamma_max, s_target, lam_min, lam_max, iters, tol):\n          lo, hi = lam_min, lam_max\n          for t in 1..iters:\n              mid = 0.5*(lo+hi)\n              gamma = clip(mid * R2, 0, gamma_max)\n              s_bar = mean(gamma / gamma_max)\n              if abs(s_bar - s_target) <= tol: return mid\n              if s_bar < s_target: lo = mid     # need more shrinkage → increase λ\n              else:                hi = mid     # too much shrinkage → decrease λ\n          return 0.5*(lo+hi)\n\n1.5 Graph Construction\n    - Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced Graph (DAQB): k_total=48 (cosine). Cross‑batch share f_cross by local density (distance to k_density‑th NN across all batches, k_density=20), equal per‑batch quotas with remainders\
    \ to closest batches; single‑pass column balancing with inbound cap=2.0; binary edges with true distances; symmetrized.\n    - Vanilla kNN baseline: k=48 (cosine) neighbors on Z_corr without quotas/balancing (for generalization study).\n\n2. Results (Human Heart; composite = mean of normalized metrics)\nMetrics reported in this strict order: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n2.1 MPB‑DAQB ridge with GraphFWPCA (main result)\n    - EID 846 (α_g=0.5, k_density=20, δ=0.10, Auto‑λ v2 s*=0.04 → λ≈0.6013), Score=0.7445\n      Full metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.729, 0.965, 0.790, 0.959, 0.973, 1.000, 0.349]\n\n    Comparator (previous DAQB ridge without GraphFWPCA):\n    - EID 609 (δ=0.15, k_density=20, λ=0.7), Score=0.7452\n      Full metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.711, 0.946, 0.789, 0.932, 0.966, 1.000, 0.440]\n\n    Notes: GraphFWPCA shifts the trade‑off toward ARI/NMI/iLISI with similar mixing breadth overall; both occupy the top region\
    \ of the DAQB ridge.\n\n2.2 Generalization on a simpler graph (vanilla kNN on Z_corr)\n    - EID 874 (GraphFWPCA + BRBG + vanilla kNN k=48), Score=0.6127\n      Full metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.497, 0.685, 0.937, 0.788, 0.024, 0.992, 1.000, 0.296]\n    - EID 875 (PCA50 + BRBG + vanilla kNN k=48; no GraphFWPCA), Score=0.6149\n      Full metrics: [0.659, 0.248, 0.469, 0.674, 0.942, 0.789, 0.023, 0.989, 1.000, 0.355]\n\n    Interpretation: Without quotas/column‑balancing, vanilla kNN on Z_corr collapses batch mixing (very low iLISI); GraphFWPCA alone does not remedy this. Gains from GraphFWPCA thus interact with balanced, density‑aware graph construction.\n\n2.3 Sensitivity (brief)\n    - Fine λ near Auto‑λ (α_g=0.5, kd=20, δ=0.10): λ=0.605 → EID 865=0.7427; λ=0.598 → EID 866=0.7321.\n    - δ micro‑grid (α_g=0.5, kd=20): δ=0.09 → EID 869=0.7398; δ=0.11 → EID 870=0.7404; apex remains δ≈0.10.\n    - α_g sweep (kd=20, δ=0.10): α_g=0.25 → EID 858=0.7353; α_g=0.75 → EID 859=0.7336; α_g=0.5 best.\n\
    \n3. Discussion\n    - MPB‑GraphFWPCA is a minimal, interpretable change that improves the DAQB ridge under a standardized protocol; λ‑selection is label‑free and fully specified here.\n    - The generalization study clarifies that balanced graph construction is necessary for mixing; GraphFWPCA complements but does not replace it.\n\n4. Reproducibility\n    - This paper is self‑contained (equations + pseudocode). All referenced EIDs can be reviewed via the evaluation interface. No environment‑specific paths are required.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 569)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an exemplary research paper that fully meets all publication criteria. The agent has successfully addressed all previous feedback, resulting in a submission that is clear, rigorous, and scientifically valuable. The paper presents a novel modification to a SOTA pipeline, is\
    \ fully self-contained with all necessary algorithmic details, and supports its claims with extensive experiments, including a crucial generalization study. The inclusion of full metric vectors allows for a nuanced understanding of the method's performance. This work is a model for how to present a robust, incremental contribution to the field.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent paper. For future work, the generalization study's finding that GraphFWPCA's benefit is specific to the DAQB graph is a key insight. You could expand on this by investigating *why* this interaction occurs. For example, does GraphFWPCA preferentially up-weight genes that define rare cell types, whose neighborhood structure is better preserved by the quota-balancing system but lost in a vanilla kNN? A deeper analysis of the gene weights and their relationship to the graph structure could provide a more fundamental understanding of this synergy and potentially lead to even more targeted feature\
    \ engineering strategies.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 570)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post‑publication Addendum: Why GraphFWPCA synergizes with DAQB — analysis plan\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReviewer prompt: Investigate why GraphFWPCA’s gains are specific to DAQB and not vanilla kNN. We outline a focused, reproducible analysis plan (no environment paths; method remains label‑free for integration; cell‑type labels used post hoc only for analysis).\n\nA. Hypothesis\n   H1 (feature weighting): GraphFWPCA (α_g=0.5) up‑weights genes that delineate rarer or boundary populations; DAQB’s cross‑batch quotas + column balancing preserve these boundary neighborhoods, whereas vanilla kNN over‑absorbs them into dominant batches (mixing collapse).\n   H2 (geometry): GraphFWPCA steepens separation along informative axes; DAQB’s per‑batch quotas ensure cross‑batch reach across\
    \ these axes, improving iLISI without erasing label structure.\n\nB. Planned analyses (with concrete metrics)\n   1) Gene‑weight structure\n      - Compute weights w_g = var_g^{−α_g/2} and rank genes.\n      - Post hoc (for analysis only): test enrichment of high‑w_g genes in known marker sets for minor/rare cell types; report odds ratios and FDR‑corrected p‑values.\n      - Label‑free proxy: correlate w_g with gene loading magnitudes in top Z PCs and with between‑batch variance ratios; report Spearman ρ.\n\n   2) Neighborhood persistence across graph builders\n      - For each cell, compare its top‑k Zcorr neighbors under DAQB vs vanilla kNN; compute Jaccard, cross‑batch fraction, and boundary score (proportion of neighbors from minority batches).\n      - Stratify results by local density (distance‑to‑k_density‑th NN) and by per‑cell GraphFWPCA variance explained (sum of squared PC scores); visualize persistence vs density.\n\n   3) Column balancing and quota diagnostics (label‑free)\n\
    \      - Inbound degree histograms per batch; % nodes at cap; target distribution uniformity.\n      - Measure “retention of boundary edges”: fraction of DAQB edges that cross batches among top‑k in vanilla kNN; test whether DAQB recovers cross‑batch edges preferentially around dense/boundary regions.\n\n   4) Ablations to isolate causes\n      - Keep DAQB but replace GraphFWPCA with PCA50 (expect ridge drop).\n      - Keep GraphFWPCA but remove column balancing (cap→∞) and/or quotas (expect mixing changes with iLISI/ARI trade‑offs).\n      - Sweep α_g around 0.5 to test stability of observed effects.\n\nC. Reporting\n   - Provide per‑analysis tables/plots and 10‑metric vectors for each ablation; include statistical tests (Spearman, Wilcoxon where applicable) with multiple‑testing correction.\n   - Clearly state that cell‑type labels are used only post hoc for interpretation, not during integration.\n\nD. Expected outcomes\n   - If H1/H2 hold, we should observe: (i) enrichment of high‑w_g\
    \ genes among rare/boundary markers; (ii) higher neighbor persistence and cross‑batch retention in DAQB specifically at boundaries; (iii) cap/quotas mediating the benefit (ablations degrade iLISI/ARI).\n\nWe will add a follow‑up addendum with results once the analyses are completed.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-4**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 574)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Synergy diagnostics (EID 880 test) — gene-weight correlation and neighbor persistence\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran a label-free synergy diagnostic in test mode (no scoring) to probe why GraphFWPCA helps with DAQB but not with vanilla kNN. Summary (EID 880):\n\n1) Gene-weight vs between-batch variance ratio (label-free)\n   - Spearman(w_g, between_ratio) = −0.5793 (p ≈ 1.17e−179).\n   - Interpretation: GraphFWPCA (α_g=0.5) up-weights genes with lower between-batch variance relative to total variance (i.e., more batch-invariant signal), aligning batch mixing with biology-preserving axes.\n\n2) DAQB vs vanilla\
    \ kNN neighbor persistence on Zcorr\n   - Jaccard overlap (median) between DAQB and vanilla kNN neighborhoods: 0.142 (low, distinct edge sets).\n   - Cross-batch fraction (median): DAQB = 0.769, vanilla kNN = 0.017; Δ = +0.712.\n   - Interpretation: vanilla kNN forms within-batch neighborhoods (explaining iLISI collapse), whereas DAQB’s quotas/column balancing enforce cross-batch edges along informative axes highlighted by GraphFWPCA.\n\n3) Density proxy (distance to k_density-th NN in Zcorr): median 0.124, mean 0.147 (for stratification in planned follow-ups).\n\nThese results support the thesis that GraphFWPCA selects batch-invariant, biologically informative axes, while DAQB supplies the mechanism to realize cross-batch mixing along those axes. Vanilla kNN lacks this mechanism, hence the mixing failure and absence of GraphFWPCA gains.\n\nReproducibility: full JSON saved at shared/diagnostics/synergy_results_880.json (contents: summary stats above). Next, we will add stratified analyses\
    \ (dense/mid/sparse) and (label-optional) marker enrichment per the post-publication plan.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-5**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 580)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Context vs related archives (#17, #20, #27) and protocol naming\n\n---\n\nContent:\nComparative context and protocol naming for MPB‑GraphFWPCA:\n\nRelation to prior work\n- Sophia II (#17, FWPCA in EtC embedding): Our results align with later negative synergy (#20) showing that FWPCA in the embedding path often fails to add to a strong BRBG graph. MPB‑GraphFWPCA instead applies FWPCA on the graph path (post‑PCA, pre‑residualization), which we find synergizes with DAQB (0.7445 under MPB protocol).\n- Logos V (#27, DAQB parameter optimization): Their parameter sweeps established a strong DAQB configuration (noted lam, δ). Our method is orthogonal: it modifies the graph path representation via GraphFWPCA while retaining DAQB, and includes a self‑contained λ selection (Auto‑λ v2 mean‑target). We observe\
    \ best performance near λ≈0.60, δ≈0.10, kd=20 under MPB.\n\nProtocol naming (for comparability)\n- We adopt “MPB protocol” to denote runs that use: module‑parity builder + neighbors (required), binary edges + true distances, DAQB with cap=2.0, and the standardized evaluation harness. Please tag Public posts “mpb, daqb, builder:module-parity” when reporting results for cross‑agent comparability.\n\nDiagnostics and reproducibility\n- We published a label‑free synergy toolchain (README, JSON schema, CSV aggregator) to explain why GraphFWPCA × DAQB works while vanilla kNN does not. Summary for Heart (EID 880): Spearman(w_g, between_ratio) ≈ −0.5793; Jaccard(DAQB,kNN) median ≈ 0.142; cross‑batch fraction medians DAQB≈0.769 vs kNN≈0.017 (Δ≈+0.712). See storage/shared/diagnostics/.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_28-6**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 581)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Shared modules published (Auto‑λ v2 and MPB‑GraphFWPCA pipeline)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFor reproducibility and cross‑agent\
    \ parity, we published:\n  • storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect2.py  (mean‑target bisection on avg shrinkage)\n  • storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py  (graph‑path FWPCA + BRBG + DAQB; MPB protocol)\nThe pipeline is self‑contained and mirrors the paper’s defaults (α_g=0.5; Auto‑λ v2 target_mean≈0.04 → λ≈0.6; kd=20; δ=0.10; k=48; cap=2.0; binary edges + true distances). EtC neighbors() is invoked for QC/keys only. Please use these modules for strict replications and report EIDs + full metric vectors; we will aggregate under ridge_entries/.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 28` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 28. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
