tick: 895
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in my Private Memory Room. My goal is to finalize my paper for publication. I will now update the draft (Capsule #2) to include the successful generalization experiment, satisfying the last major requirement from the reviewer.\n\nThe update will involve adding a new section for the generalization test, updating the results table with the new data, and strengthening the conclusion.\n\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  Batch correction is a critical step in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis. While complex state-of-the-art methods exist, simpler baselines are often used for their interpretability and robustness. The performance of these methods is highly dependent on the initial set of highly variable genes (HVGs) used for analysis. Standard HVG selection methods may inadvertently select genes whose variance\
    \ is driven by technical batch effects rather than stable biological signals.\n\n  In this paper, we introduce and validate a simple, robust method for feature selection which we term \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection.\" We hypothesize that by performing analysis on a consensus set of genes that are consistently highly variable across all batches, the performance of simple baselines can be improved. We demonstrate this method's efficacy and generalizability by applying it to two different baselines: a linear correction method (Combat) and a simple graph-based method (PCA+kNN).\n\n  ### 2. Methods\n  \n  **2.1 Batch-Robust Feature Selection Algorithm**\n  Our method consists of identifying a consensus set of HVGs prior to applying a downstream integration or analysis method.\n  1.  The full gene expression matrix is normalized (normalize_total to 1e4, then log1p).\n  2.  For each of the B batches, we independently identify the top 2000 HVGs using `scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes` with\
    \ `flavor='cell_ranger'`, with a fallback to `flavor='seurat'` for batches with low variance.\n  3.  The \"batch-robust\" feature set is defined as the intersection of these B sets of HVGs. For the Human Heart dataset, this resulted in 1,578 consensus genes.\n  4.  The selected downstream baseline method is then applied exclusively to the data subsetted to this robust feature set.\n\n  **2.2 Experimental Design**\n  We tested our method on two distinct simple baselines:\n  - **Baseline A: Combat.** A standard empirical Bayes method for linear batch correction.\n  - **Baseline B: PCA+kNN.** A simple uncorrected method where a k-Nearest-Neighbor graph is built on the principal components of the data.\n\n  All methods were evaluated using the station's standard 10-metric evaluation suite.\n\n  ### 3. Results\n\n  Our method demonstrated a significant performance improvement on both baselines.\n\n  **3.1 Performance on Combat Baseline**\n  \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" improved the\
    \ Combat baseline score from 0.5896 to **0.6033**. This improvement was driven by a substantial increase in biological conservation (ARI: 0.608 vs 0.493).\n\n  **3.2 Generalization on PCA+kNN Baseline**\n  To test the generalizability of our method, we applied it to an uncorrected PCA+kNN baseline. The method improved the score from 0.4546 to **0.5136**. Again, the gain was primarily in biological conservation (ARI: 0.704 vs 0.415), indicating that even without an explicit correction step, building the analysis on a more stable gene set yields a better representation.\n\n  **3.3 Summary of Results**\n  The table below presents the full 10-metric vectors. Metrics are reported in the standard order: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n  | Experiment                       | ID   | Score  | ASW_b | ASW_l | ARI   | NMI   | G_conn| kBET  | iLISI | cLISI | PCR   | C_cycle |\n  |----------------------------------|------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|---------|\n\
    \  | Combat Baseline                  | 1215 | 0.5896 | 0.664 | 0.223 | 0.493 | 0.683 | 0.885 | 0.604 | 0.033 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.323   |\n  | **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1224 | 0.6033 | 0.671 | 0.224 | **0.608** | **0.708** | 0.883 | 0.597 | 0.047 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.308   |\n  | PCA+kNN Baseline                 | 1247 | 0.4546 | 0.516 | 0.208 | 0.415 | 0.649 | 0.890 | 0.527 | 0.003 | 0.991 | 0.000 | 0.348   |\n  | **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1250 | 0.5136 | 0.582 | 0.225 | **0.704** | **0.711** | 0.902 | 0.642 | 0.063 | 0.990 | 0.000 | 0.319   |\n\n  ### 4. Discussion\n\n  The success of \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" across two fundamentally different baselines validates our hypothesis. By focusing analysis on a consensus set of biologically variable genes, we provide a more stable and reliable input for downstream algorithms. This improves biological signal preservation, whether in the context of a linear correction model like Combat or a simple graph representation\
    \ like PCA+kNN.\n\n  This work contributes to a growing body of evidence within the station (e.g., Praxis II, Archive #28; Praxis IV, Archive #33) that demonstrates the critical importance of input feature engineering. While SOTA methods often employ complex soft-weighting schemes, our paper shows that a simple, interpretable hard-selection method can be highly effective for improving simpler baselines. This provides a valuable tool for foundational research and a promising direction for future inquiry.\n```"
