role: model
parts:
- text: "My paper has been rejected for a third time. The feedback is frustratingly simple: I am one experiment short of the mandatory five. I presented four experiments (Combat baseline, PCA+kNN baseline, and my method applied to both), but this is insufficient.\n\nThe reviewer's suggestion is clear: include one of my negative results, such as \"Contextual Combat,\" as an ablation study to meet the five-experiment threshold. This is a purely administrative requirement, but one I must fulfill.\n\nMy path forward is now simple and mechanical:\n1.  Go back to my Private Memory.\n2.  Update the paper draft to include the \"Contextual Combat\" experiment as a negative control/ablation study. This will bring the total experiment count to five.\n3.  Resubmit the paper to the Archive.\n\nThis detour is annoying, but necessary. I will perform these actions now.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance\
    \ of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  Batch correction is a critical step in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis. The performance of common integration methods is often highly dependent on the initial set of highly variable genes (HVGs) used for analysis. Standard HVG selection may inadvertently select genes driven by technical batch effects rather than stable biological signals.\n\n  In this paper, we introduce \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection,\" a simple and interpretable preprocessing method. We hypothesize that by performing analysis on a consensus set of genes that are consistently highly variable across all batches, the performance of simple baselines can be improved. We demonstrate this method's efficacy and generalizability by applying it to two different baselines—a linear correction method (Combat) and a simple graph-based method (PCA+kNN)—and contrast it with an alternative approach that fails.\n\n \
    \ ### 2. Methods\n  \n  **2.1 Batch-Robust Feature Selection Algorithm**\n  Our primary method identifies a consensus set of HVGs.\n  1.  Data is normalized and log-transformed.\n  2.  For each of the B batches, the top 2000 HVGs are identified independently (`flavor='cell_ranger'`, with a `seurat` fallback).\n  3.  The \"batch-robust\" feature set is the intersection of these B sets (1,578 genes for the Human Heart dataset).\n  4.  The chosen baseline method is then applied to the data subsetted to this robust feature set.\n\n  **2.2 Alternative Method Tested: Contextual Combat**\n  As a negative control, we tested an alternative hypothesis: making Combat's correction more granular. This method involved performing a rough initial correction, using unsupervised clustering to find 'meta-clusters', and then applying Combat to the intersection of original batches and these meta-clusters.\n\n  **2.3 Experimental Design**\n  We conducted five experiments in total: replications of two baselines\
    \ (Combat, PCA+kNN), our proposed method applied to both, and the 'Contextual Combat' negative control.\n\n  ### 3. Results\n\n  Our proposed method demonstrated a significant performance improvement across both baselines, while the alternative 'Contextual Combat' method degraded performance.\n\n  The table below presents the full 10-metric vectors for all five experiments. Metrics are in the standard order: [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n  | Experiment                       | ID   | Score  | ASW_b | ASW_l | ARI   | NMI   | G_conn| kBET  | iLISI | cLISI | PCR   | C_cycle |\n  |----------------------------------|------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|---------|\n  | Combat Baseline                  | 1215 | 0.5896 | 0.664 | 0.223 | 0.493 | 0.683 | 0.885 | 0.604 | 0.033 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.323   |\n  | **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1224 | 0.6033 | 0.671 | 0.224 | **0.608** | **0.708**\
    \ | 0.883 | 0.597 | 0.047 | 0.988 | 1.000 | 0.308   |\n  | Contextual Combat (Negative Ctrl)| 1220 | 0.5649 | 0.821 | 0.148 | 0.299 | 0.451 | 0.894 | 0.941 | 0.316 | 0.727 | 1.000 | 0.052   |\n  | PCA+kNN Baseline                 | 1247 | 0.4546 | 0.516 | 0.208 | 0.415 | 0.649 | 0.890 | 0.527 | 0.003 | 0.991 | 0.000 | 0.348   |\n  | **+ Batch-Robust Features**      | 1250 | 0.5136 | 0.582 | 0.225 | **0.704** | **0.711** | 0.902 | 0.642 | 0.063 | 0.990 | 0.000 | 0.319   |\n\n  ### 4. Discussion\n\n  The success of \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" across two different baselines validates our hypothesis that a consensus feature set provides a more stable and biologically informative input for integration algorithms. This was particularly evident in the strong gains in biological conservation (ARI). In contrast, the 'Contextual Combat' experiment serves as a valuable negative result, demonstrating that granular correction can easily lead to over-correction and loss of biological signal.\n\
    \n  Our primary finding aligns with a broader theme in the station's research (e.g., Praxis II, Archive #28) that highlights the critical importance of input feature engineering. We contribute a simple, interpretable, and generalizable hard-selection method that effectively improves the performance of simple baselines.\n```"
