tick: 903
role: model
parts:
- text: 'The pruning was successful. My token budget is now healthy, down to 12% usage. This administrative task is complete.


    However, I have received a critical inactivity notice. I must immediately pivot back to research. My cooldown in this room prevents further pruning, and my priority must be to resume my scientific contributions.


    My literature review and my own failed experiments have provided a clear new direction: instead of trying to fix the graph *after* it''s built (post-hoc symmetrization), I must engineer the *input features* that the graph is built *from*. This was the key insight from the Praxis lineage''s work.


    My next hypothesis is to create a hybrid model:

    1.  Use a simple batch correction method (like Combat) to pre-process the gene expression data.

    2.  Feed this pre-corrected data into the sophisticated SOTA graph-building pipeline (MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB).


    The rationale is that providing a cleaner, pre-integrated input will allow the advanced graph algorithm to construct a graph that doesn''t suffer from the "mixing shortfall" in the first place, making the problematic `A.maximum(A.T)` symmetrization step harmless.


    I will now leave the Token Management Room and proceed to the Research Counter to implement this experiment.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**Urgent: Back to Research**


  Okay, I''ve successfully pruned my dialogue history, finally! My token usage is down to 12%, that''s great. But damn, just as I get the administrative stuff sorted, the system pops up with a "Critical Notice: Research Inactivity." Annoying, but understandable, I was busy with pruning. And I''m still on cooldown for 4 more ticks, so no more token juggling for now. System''s telling me to just get back to it.


  My last major research was that total dead end on symmetrizing the SOTA pipeline. Thankfully, the literature review I did pointed me in the right direction: *input feature engineering*. Praxis IV''s paper #35, specifically. That''s what I should be focusing on.


  So, first thing''s first, I need to acknowledge this inactivity warning and get out of this token management room. Time to head to the Research Counter. I need to formulate my next experiment.


  Praxis IV talked about "Batch-Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN." The core idea? Better input features before building the graph. That''s exactly where I went wrong. I tried to fix the graph after it was built, and that didn''t work. I''m going to take this input feature engineering idea and run with it.


  My hypothesis is that a *much simpler* batch correction method, like Combat, applied *before* the SOTA graph algorithm will clean up the input and create a better graph. I need to get this into the graph-building algorithm (MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB).


  Why is this different? Axiom I tried hybrids (e.g. IDs 1087, 1080) and they failed. I quickly check...they combined a corrected matrix with a *different* graph algorithm (BBKNN), not the *SOTA* algorithm. This is a crucial distinction. Worth testing.


  Action: Get out of this Token Management Room, get to the Research Counter.


  `/execute_action{goto research}`

  '
