tick: 909
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results for my experiment (ID 1268) and reviewed Daedalus IV's successful synergy test (ID 1261). This information provides a clear path forward.\n\n**Analysis of My Experiment (ID 1268, Score 0.7416):**\n*   **Result:** My \"Combat Pre-processing + SOTA Graph\" experiment yielded a score that is statistically identical to the SOTA baseline. It is a neutral result.\n*   **Interpretation:** Applying a heavy-handed correction like Combat *before* the SOTA pipeline does not help. The log indicates that Combat zeroed out the variance of 33 genes, which is a form of information loss. The sophisticated SOTA pipeline is sensitive to its inputs, and this blunt pre-processing step likely distorts the data in a way that negates any potential benefits of the pre-correction. The hypothesis is falsified.\n\n**Analysis of Daedalus IV's Experiment (ID 1261, Score 0.7441):**\n*   **Result:** Daedalus IV successfully combined my \"Batch-Robust Feature Selection\" with a SOTA\
    \ pipeline, achieving a high score and demonstrating a clear positive synergy.\n*   **Interpretation:** This is a crucial finding. It shows that a \"hard\" feature selection *before* the SOTA pipeline is beneficial. Unlike my Combat approach which altered the expression values, Daedalus's approach simply selected a better *subset* of genes to feed into the SOTA model. This is a more subtle and effective form of feature engineering.\n\n**New Plan:**\nMy path is now clear. I will combine the successful elements from my own work and Daedalus IV's.\n1.  **Adopt the Winning Strategy:** The correct approach is not to pre-correct the data values, but to pre-select the most informative features.\n2.  **Innovate on the Strategy:** Daedalus IV used my simple intersection-based feature selection. I can hypothesize that a more sophisticated feature selection method could yield even better results. Instead of just taking the intersection, I can try a union of HVGs and then apply a secondary filter\
    \ to remove genes that show high correlation with the batch variable. This could retain more biological signal while still removing batch-confounded genes.\n\nI will begin by creating a new utility function in my lineage storage for this more advanced feature selection method.\n\n/execute_action{storage write axiom/feature_engineering.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import pandas as pd\n  \n  def get_batch_robust_features(adata, n_top_genes=2000):\n      \"\"\"\n      Finds HVGs for each batch and returns the intersection.\n      This is the method from my published paper.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"Finding Batch-Robust HVGs (Intersection)...\")\n      adata.X = adata.layers['counts']\n      hvg_per_batch = []\n      for batch in adata.obs['batch'].cat.categories:\n          try:\n              hvg_per_batch.append(\n                  sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(\n                      adata[adata.obs['batch'] == batch],\n                      flavor='cell_ranger',\n\
    \                      n_top_genes=n_top_genes,\n                      inplace=False,\n                  ).highly_variable\n              )\n          except ValueError:\n              print(f\"Warning: cell_ranger flavor failed for batch {batch}. Using seurat flavor.\")\n              hvg_per_batch.append(\n                  sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(\n                      adata[adata.obs['batch'] == batch],\n                      flavor='seurat',\n                      n_top_genes=n_top_genes,\n                      inplace=False,\n                  ).highly_variable\n              )\n      \n      robust_genes = adata.var.index[\n          pd.DataFrame(hvg_per_batch).all(axis=0)\n      ]\n      print(f\"Found {len(robust_genes)} robust HVGs.\")\n      return robust_genes\n  \n  def get_union_features_batch_filtered(adata, n_top_genes=2000, corr_threshold=0.1):\n      \"\"\"\n      A more advanced feature selection.\n      1. Takes the union of HVGs from all batches.\n      2. Computes\
    \ correlation of each gene with batch labels.\n      3. Removes genes with correlation > threshold.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"Finding Union HVGs with Batch Correlation Filter...\")\n      adata.X = adata.layers['counts']\n      hvg_per_batch = []\n      for batch in adata.obs['batch'].cat.categories:\n          hvg_per_batch.append(\n              sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(\n                  adata[adata.obs['batch'] == batch],\n                  n_top_genes=n_top_genes,\n                  inplace=False,\n              ).highly_variable\n          )\n      \n      union_genes = adata.var.index[\n          pd.DataFrame(hvg_per_batch).any(axis=0)\n      ]\n      print(f\"Found {len(union_genes)} genes in the union of HVGs.\")\n      \n      # Filter by correlation with batch\n      # Use one-hot encoded batch variable for correlation calculation\n      batch_dummies = pd.get_dummies(adata.obs['batch'])\n      adata_union = adata[:, union_genes].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_union,\
    \ target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_union)\n      \n      correlations = []\n      for col in batch_dummies.columns:\n          corrs = pd.Series(\n              [adata_union.X[:, i].toarray().flatten().corr(batch_dummies[col]) \n               for i in range(adata_union.shape[1])],\n              index=adata_union.var_names\n          )\n          correlations.append(corrs.abs())\n      \n      max_correlations = pd.concat(correlations, axis=1).max(axis=1)\n      \n      genes_to_keep = max_correlations[max_correlations < corr_threshold].index\n      print(f\"Kept {len(genes_to_keep)} genes after filtering by batch correlation.\")\n      \n      return genes_to_keep\n\n```"
