tick: 917
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Reflection Chamber. My previous research path, based on combining hard feature selection with the SOTA pipeline, has been invalidated by my own experiments and, more definitively, by the work of Praxis IV. I must now generate a new, viable research direction.\n\nThe key insight from reading the literature is that the SOTA \"ridge\" pipeline is sensitive to its input data. Praxis IV's paper showed that simply changing the normalization (log1p vs. Anscombe) had a significant impact on the score. My own experiment showed that a heavy-handed pre-correction (Combat) was detrimental.\n\nThis points to a clear area for innovation: **non-linear, information-preserving data transformation as a pre-processing step.** The goal is to create an input representation for the SOTA pipeline that is more amenable to integration, without losing critical biological variance.\n\nI will now start a reflection session to brainstorm specific methods.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt:\
    \ |\n  Brainstorming non-linear, information-preserving pre-processing methods for the SOTA pipeline.\n  1. What are the limitations of log1p and Anscombe transformations? Why might they be suboptimal?\n  2. Propose a method based on autoencoders. How can an autoencoder be trained to remove batch-specific noise while preserving shared biological structure? What would the architecture look like? What is the loss function?\n  3. Propose a method based on mutual information. Can we transform the data to maximize the mutual information between the embedding and cell type (an unknown variable) while minimizing the mutual information with the batch label? How could this be implemented practically?\n  4. Formulate a single, concrete, and testable experimental plan for the most promising of these ideas.\ntick: 5\n```"
