tick: 924
role: model
parts:
- text: "My grand experiment has failed. The debugger's report is unambiguous and insightful. The failure is not a simple bug but a fundamental design incompatibility. My hypothesis has not been tested, only my implementation has been proven flawed.\n\n**Analysis of Failure (ID 1284):**\n*   **Root Cause:** A critical oversight on my part. I designed a 50-dimensional latent space with the AAE but attempted to feed it into a SOTA pipeline that is hard-coded to expect high-dimensional gene data from which it can extract **60** PCA components. This is mathematically impossible.\n*   **Secondary Flaw:** The SOTA pipeline also expects non-negative count data for its normalization steps, whereas my AAE latent space contains negative values and is already a transformed representation. My approach was conceptually unsound from a data-type perspective.\n*   **Lesson Learned:** I must be more diligent in analyzing the exact input requirements and internal workings of any pre-existing code I intend\
    \ to use, especially when it is read-only. Ambition must be matched with rigorous verification.\n\n**The Path Forward:**\nThe debugger's report provides an excellent and logical path forward. The core idea—evaluating the quality of the AAE's batch-corrected embedding—is still valid. I simply chose the wrong tool to build the final graph. The debugger's **Option A** is the most direct and scientifically pure way to test my hypothesis: build a standard k-NN graph directly on the AAE embedding and see how it performs.\n\nI will now implement this recommendation. This new experiment will cleanly evaluate whether my AAE can produce a representation that is superior for downstream graph-based analysis, without the confounding factors of the incompatible SOTA pipeline.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Direct Graph Construction on AAE Latent Embedding\"\ntags: \"autoencoder, adversarial, graph-construction, non-linear, embedding\"\nabstract: \"Previous attempt (ID 1284) to pipe\
    \ an AAE embedding into the SOTA pipeline failed due to a fundamental dimensionality mismatch. This experiment follows the debugger's recommendation to directly test the quality of the AAE's 50-dimensional latent embedding. We will generate the embedding using the pre-trained encoder (from ID 1283) and then construct a standard k-NN graph directly upon it using scanpy.pp.neighbors. This provides a clean evaluation of the AAE pre-processing method's ability to generate a superior representation for graph-based integration.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax\n  import pickle\n  \n  # Set JAX to CPU\n  import os\n  os.environ['JAX_PLATFORMS'] = 'cpu'\n  \n  # Import model definitions\n  sys.path.append('storage/axiom')\n  from aae_model import get_models\n  \n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Generates AAE embedding and builds a k-NN graph\
    \ directly on it.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Running Direct Graph Construction on AAE Latent Space ---\")\n      \n      # --- 1. Load Pre-trained Encoder ---\n      n_features = adata.shape[1]\n      n_batches = len(adata.obs['batch'].cat.categories)\n      encoder, _, _ = get_models(n_input_features=n_features, n_batches=n_batches)\n      \n      with open(\"storage/axiom/trained_encoder.pkl\", \"rb\") as f:\n          encoder_params = pickle.load(f)\n      \n      # Re-initialize the model structure before loading state dict\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      initial_params = encoder.init(key, jnp.ones((1, n_features)))['params']\n      encoder_params = flax.serialization.from_state_dict(initial_params, encoder_params)\n      \n      # --- 2. Prepare Data and Generate Embedding ---\n      adata_norm = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_norm, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_norm)\n      X_norm = adata_norm.X.toarray() if hasattr(adata_norm.X, 'toarray')\
    \ else adata_norm.X\n      \n      print(\"Generating latent embedding from pre-trained encoder...\")\n      latent_embedding = encoder.apply({'params': encoder_params}, X_norm)\n      print(f\"Generated embedding of shape: {latent_embedding.shape}\")\n      \n      # --- 3. Build Graph Directly on the Latent Embedding ---\n      adata_result = adata.copy()\n      # Place the AAE embedding into the obsm slot for sc.pp.neighbors\n      adata_result.obsm['X_emb'] = latent_embedding\n      \n      print(\"Building k-NN graph directly on the AAE embedding...\")\n      # Use a standard neighbor count, similar to the SOTA baseline\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata_result, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=50)\n      \n      # The embedding itself is also a valid output for evaluation\n      # The evaluation metrics will use the graph if present, which it is.\n      \n      return adata_result\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Learning Moment: Pivoting After a Design Flaw**\n\nWell, this is frustrating, but a valuable learning experience nonetheless. My initial ambition to create this hybrid AAE-SOTA graph pipeline (Experiment ID 1284) has hit a brick wall. The debugger's analysis confirmed my worst fears: it's not a simple bug; it's a fundamental design incompatibility. I overlooked a crucial detail: the SOTA pipeline I was leveraging (`praxis/best_mpb_daqb_kd22_k50.py`) is hard-coded to produce 60 PCA components, while my AAE only outputs a 50-dimensional latent space. That's a no-go from the get-go. And, to top it off, the latent space can have negative values, which conflicts with another expected pre-processing step in the pipeline.\n\nI can't even modify the SOTA code, which makes it even more challenging. The debugger's provided options (A, B, and C) have forced me to re-evaluate my approach. I had a hypothesis, that the AAE produces better representations. And this failure *doesn't*\
  \ refute that hypothesis, it just means I picked the wrong tools to test it. \n\nI've decided to go with **Option A**: Build a graph *directly* on the 50-dimensional AAE embedding using a standard k-NN algorithm. This seems like the most logical and efficient route. It will provide a clean evaluation of the AAE's ability to produce a graph that reflects meaningful relationships in the data. No more entangled pre-processing steps. It also keeps my core concept intact, and I can iterate rapidly.\n\nI need to acknowledge this misstep and reframe the experiment. My thought process was too ambitious and not cautious enough in inspecting the SOTA pipeline's requirements. This pivot is critical and scientifically sound. I'll load the encoder, generate the embedding, then get the embedding into `adata.obsm['X_emb']` and perform a basic k-NN graph.\n\nThis is the only rational path forward. This will determine if the AAE is a good representation, which is the heart of the original goal.\n"
