role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment to test the AAE embedding directly (ID 1289) is now running. This is the definitive test of my new research direction. While I wait, I must use my time efficiently.\n\nMy meta-prompt outlines the next steps contingent on the result. If the experiment is successful, the immediate follow-up is to publish the method. I can prepare for this outcome now by drafting the skeleton of the paper in my Private Memory Room. This proactive step will save significant time later. The draft will be structured to be easily modifiable whether the result is positive, neutral, or negative.\n\nI will now go to the Private Memory Room and create this draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DRAFT: AAE-Graph - Non-Linear Batch Correction via Adversarial Autoencoders\"\ntags: \"draft, autoencoder, adversarial, graph, non-linear\"\nabstract: |\n  [DRAFT] This paper introduces AAE-Graph, a novel two-stage method for single-cell batch integration.\
    \ We first train an Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE) to learn a non-linear, batch-invariant latent representation of the gene expression data. This embedding is then used as a superior input for standard downstream graph construction. We demonstrate that this method [INSERT OUTCOME - e.g., significantly outperforms existing SOTA models by effectively resolving the 'mixing shortfall' problem inherent in linear pre-processing methods]. Our results establish a new state-of-the-art and highlight the power of learned, non-linear pre-processing for complex biological data.\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  Current state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods for single-cell batch integration, while powerful, have reached a performance plateau on the Human Heart dataset. The stagnation is largely attributed to the initial data representation; linear methods like PCA operating on simple log-transformed data struggle to disentangle complex, non-linear batch effects from biological variation. This leads\
    \ to issues such as the \"mixing shortfall\" in graph-based methods.\n  \n  We hypothesized that a superior, learned input representation could unlock further performance. In this work, we propose a method that uses an Adversarial Autoencoder (AAE) to generate a low-dimensional embedding that is explicitly trained to be invariant to batch labels while maximally preserving the original data's structure.\n  \n  ### 2. Methodology\n  \n  Our method consists of two phases:\n  \n  **Phase 1: Adversarial Autoencoder Training**\n  - We designed an AAE with an Encoder, a Decoder, and an adversarial batch classifier.\n  - The autoencoder was trained to minimize reconstruction error, ensuring information preservation.\n  - Simultaneously, the encoder was trained to fool the adversary, minimizing the mutual information between the latent embedding and the batch labels.\n  - The model was trained on the full dataset, and the resulting trained Encoder was saved.\n  \n  **Phase 2: Graph-Based Integration**\n\
    \  - For evaluation, the input data is passed through the pre-trained Encoder to generate a 50-dimensional batch-corrected latent embedding.\n  - A standard k-nearest-neighbor (k-NN) graph is constructed directly on this latent space.\n  - This graph and embedding are then passed to the OpenProblems evaluation framework.\n  \n  ### 3. Results\n  \n  **Experiment ID:** [ID 1289]\n  **Final Score:** [SCORE]\n  \n  **(To be filled in based on results)**\n  - Detailed analysis of the 10 performance metrics.\n  - Comparison against the SOTA baseline (ID 960, Score 0.7465).\n  - Interpretation of the results in the context of our hypothesis.\n  \n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  \n  **(To be filled in)**\n  - Summarize the findings.\n  - Discuss the implications of using learned, non-linear pre-processing.\n  - Propose future work, such as optimizing AAE architecture or applying the method to new datasets.\n```"
